WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:24.200
Welcome everybody to this training. In the first part of this training there will be an introduction on the heel system and why it's important to use an heel, why it's necessary to use an heel and in the second part there will be a training on control desk, how to use control desk.

00:24.200 --> 00:45.700
The first question is why is heel important, so why it's needed to have a heel simulation. The answer to this question is because the heel simulation allows you to link together computer simulation and vehicle testing.

00:45.700 --> 00:59.700
So the heel gives you the possibility to have a real-time application while using real components like for example sensor and actuators.

00:59.700 --> 01:12.700
The application that runs on the heel is composed basically of physics-based or mathematical models.

01:12.700 --> 01:31.700
So starting from the mathematical models we create a Simulink model. Simulink model is then translated into code. The code is run on the application.

01:31.700 --> 01:44.700
We can imagine in the real world to have another in the loop like in the first picture. In the first picture there is the controller and the process.

01:44.700 --> 02:08.700
The controller, the role of the controller is to control the inputs to the process, then read the output from the process, then calculate an error and on the base of this error adjust the inputs to give to the process.

02:08.700 --> 02:21.700
In the simulation we can recreate the real world to have the hardware as controller and then the simulation instead of the real process.

02:21.700 --> 02:33.700
So the simulation recreates the behavior of the process while the hardware is responsible for the controller.

02:33.700 --> 02:44.700
Now let's see together what are the advantages of using a heel.

02:44.700 --> 03:01.700
Of course the heel reduce the cost of testing. Let's imagine that if we want to test on a mule the costs are higher than testing on a heel system.

03:01.700 --> 03:07.700
Another important aspect of the heel is the reproducibility. What does it mean?

03:07.700 --> 03:24.700
It means that it is possible to reproduce, for example, a bug. If we found a bug, having the heel gives the possibility to reproduce the same error many times.

03:24.700 --> 03:34.700
Because we are able to recreate the conditions to let the bug appear.

03:34.700 --> 03:48.700
Another main aspect of the heel usage is the safety. Some tests are not safe to be done on a real vehicle.

03:48.700 --> 03:58.700
Let's imagine a short battery on the accelerator pedal. It has a high level of danger.

03:58.700 --> 04:16.700
So in this case we perform the same maneuver on a hill. So in this case we lower the possibility, we reduce the possibility to have dangerous condition for the driver to zero.

04:16.700 --> 04:41.700
On the other hand, there can be some critical aspects. One critical aspect is to develop an accurate model of the real plant. In other words, we have to model the real plant as close as possible to the reality.

04:41.700 --> 05:10.700
Meaning that it is possible to recreate the same conditions as in the real vehicle. The more the model is accurate, so the more the model is close to the real plant, the better will be the test we are able to perform and the better are the results that we're going to have.

05:10.700 --> 05:29.700
Let's analyze now a hill architecture. In yellow you will find what are the simulated parts in a hill architecture. While in green there will be the real hardware present in a hill architecture.

05:29.700 --> 05:47.700
The real components more often are sensors and actuators. It is possible to simulate sensors and actuators and it is also possible to have real sensors and actuators.

05:47.700 --> 05:58.700
Then usually there is one or multiple issues under test. So the real issue is connected to the hill, to the simulator.

05:58.700 --> 06:26.700
Other issues are most often simulated because there is a percentage of hill architecture that don't have all the issues real. For your information, there are some architectures that have all the real issues.

06:26.700 --> 06:45.700
Like for example, like for example, lab car. Then there is the physical system. So the model that recreates the plant that runs on the simulator.

06:45.700 --> 07:11.700
Following the arrows, you can see the exchange of information. For example, the physical system gives inputs to the sensors. The sensors gives output to the issue under test that analyze the inputs and creates some outputs in two other issues.

07:11.700 --> 07:39.700
And they also receive information from other issues. But the main important thing about hill architecture is the issue that provides output to the actuators. Then the actuators gives a feedback to the physical system. So this is how we close the loop in a hill architecture.

07:41.700 --> 08:00.700
This is a typical system. This is a typical system architecture in which we have the PC, on which we have some tools. In this case, there is an example of tool from this space, control desk, and also, for example, configuration desk.

08:00.700 --> 08:07.700
The connection between the PC, configuration desk. The connection between the PC and the Skalexio is made usually through an Ethernet.

08:07.700 --> 08:14.700
Then there is the connection between the ECU, the ECU, the ECU, the ECU under test, for example, one or multiple.

08:14.700 --> 08:37.700
This connection, for example, this connection, for example, is made of I.O. channels. The I.O. can have multiple shapes, for example, can be an analog, I.O., digital I.O., resistive, PBM, engine simulation, and so on.

08:37.700 --> 08:47.700
So, the Skalexio exchanges information with real ECU, so sends, for example, inputs to the real ECU.

08:47.700 --> 08:58.700
The ECU elaborates using the software flashed on the ECU. The information coming from the Skalexio creates some output that is sent back to the Skalexio.

08:58.700 --> 09:16.700
The PC is responsible for the part of configuration of the Skalexio, but this is not the main topic of this lesson, and also for control desk.

09:16.700 --> 09:21.700
Control desk just gives you an interface to the model.

09:21.700 --> 09:30.700
So, using control desk on our PC, we can have an interface with the model running on the Skalexio.

09:30.700 --> 09:41.700
So, we are going to be able to see inputs and outputs of the model running on the Skalexio.

09:41.700 --> 09:58.700
It's important to specify that, with respect to the simulation, in this system architecture, so using Skalexio, we have a real-time application running on the target.

09:58.700 --> 10:04.700
In fact, the real-time application is running on Skalexio.

10:04.700 --> 10:06.700
Let's analyze these pictures together.

10:06.700 --> 10:18.700
There is the Skalexio in the middle, which has IO hardware, fault injection units, loads for unit under test, processing hardware, and battery simulation.

10:18.700 --> 10:20.700
So, the power supply unit.

10:20.700 --> 10:29.700
The Skalexio exchanges information with the device under test, so the ECU, for example, and to the simulation model.

10:29.700 --> 10:34.700
The simulation model is the model loaded inside the Skalexio.

10:34.700 --> 10:49.700
Then, we have the possibility to interface with Skalexio using, as already said, control desk, but also other platforms, like automation desk for test automation,

10:49.700 --> 10:57.700
model desk for model parameterization, motion desk, and also third-party software.

10:57.700 --> 11:13.700
Before moving to the second part of this training, so how to use control desk, let's reassume what are the features of a Skalexio system.

11:13.700 --> 11:20.700
The first important thing is the run of the real-time application.

11:20.700 --> 11:36.700
So, the hill, with respect to the sealed mill, which are software in the loop and model in the loop, gives the possibility to have real-time application running.

11:36.700 --> 11:45.700
The Skalexio, through the boards, can generate sensor signals to the ECU.

11:45.700 --> 11:55.700
So, generates the outputs of the Skalexio that are inputs to the ECU.

11:55.700 --> 12:08.700
At the same time, it can reach command signals from actuators and also read inputs of the ECU.

12:08.700 --> 12:11.700
Inputs from the ECU, sorry.

12:11.700 --> 12:14.700
It provides battery voltage to the ECU.

12:14.700 --> 12:22.700
So, each ECU needs to have battery voltage, so the power supply to work.

12:22.700 --> 12:33.700
The Skalexio provides to the ECUs the battery voltage to work.

12:33.700 --> 12:40.700
And then, another important thing is that the Skalexio is able to simulate faults.

12:40.700 --> 12:44.700
FIU stands for Fault Injection Unit.

12:44.700 --> 12:58.700
So, using the Skalexio, it's possible to simulate faults and then analyze the behavior of the ECU when those faults are simulated.

12:58.700 --> 13:05.700
Now, let's move on to Control Desk.

13:05.700 --> 13:14.700
Control Desk is a tool from this space that gives you the possibility to directly interface with the model.

13:14.700 --> 13:25.700
So, as you already said, the model is present and is running on the real-time application on the Skalexio.

13:25.700 --> 13:32.700
Control Desk is the interface from the user to the model.

13:32.700 --> 13:52.700
So, using Control Desk, it's possible, for example, to change inputs, analyze the outputs, having access to the bus for CAN connection, link connection, ethernet connections, and so on.

13:52.700 --> 13:59.700
Using Control Desk is possible to access calibration, measuring, and diagnostics of the ECU.

13:59.700 --> 14:09.700
So, when the ECU is connected to the real-time application, using Control Desk is possible to perform some tests.

14:09.700 --> 14:12.700
So, let's imagine there is a new software release.

14:12.700 --> 14:15.700
The software is flashed on the ECU.

14:15.700 --> 14:17.700
We want to test the software.

14:17.700 --> 14:25.700
We connect the ECU to the HILD simulator and then we are able, using Control Desk, to perform maneuvers.

14:25.700 --> 14:43.700
And see how the new software behaves, so how the software behaves after these maneuvers are performed.

14:43.700 --> 14:46.700
Now, let's move on to Control Desk.

14:46.700 --> 14:53.700
So, in this case, I'm going to show you an application, a real-time application.

14:53.700 --> 15:06.700
So, we're going to see how to use Control Desk, what is possible to do, what is possible to see and to analyze using Control Desk.

15:06.700 --> 15:18.700
So, this is the window that you have as soon as you open Control Desk.

15:18.700 --> 15:26.700
For each application, this window may change, it will change, basically.

15:26.700 --> 15:37.700
But, it's important to have clear in mind that even if the layout is different, what is possible to achieve is the same.

15:37.700 --> 15:41.700
So, the functionalities of Control Desk are the same.

15:41.700 --> 15:47.700
First step is to go online.

15:47.700 --> 15:54.700
Go online means that we're going to start the online calibration for Control Desk.

15:54.700 --> 15:57.700
So, we run the application.

15:57.700 --> 16:04.700
The application, after going online, is running on the simulator.

16:04.700 --> 16:10.700
So, we're going to see the real-time behavior of the model.

16:10.700 --> 16:27.700
As you can see here, as soon as I went online, this variable started to change, because they are representing the real behavior of these components, in this case, the TVA.

16:27.700 --> 16:34.700
Second thing to do is to give battery to the ECU.

16:34.700 --> 16:46.700
As already said, the Skalexio system has the possibility to give power supply to the ECU.

16:46.700 --> 16:53.700
In this case, I'm going to click here and have the main supply active.

16:53.700 --> 17:01.700
In this case, this is 24 volts, but usually you will find 12, 14 volts.

17:01.700 --> 17:02.700
It's the same.

17:02.700 --> 17:03.700
It's the same.

17:03.700 --> 17:08.700
In my application, the level of battery is 24.

17:08.700 --> 17:16.700
Then, I'm going to show you how to flash using JTAG.

17:16.700 --> 17:23.700
JTAG memtool is a program to flash the ECU.

17:23.700 --> 17:27.700
Flashing the ECU is possible using Inca and so on.

17:27.700 --> 17:30.700
In my application, I flash using JTAG.

17:30.700 --> 17:41.700
It's important that I show you these because it's possible to understand that there are many ways to flash an ECU.

17:41.700 --> 17:46.700
In this case, I go on key on.

17:46.700 --> 17:50.700
I click here to start flashing.

17:50.700 --> 17:57.700
I select my ECU type and then I'm able to flash.

17:57.700 --> 18:05.700
For example, in this case, I take the hex file and I'm able to flash.

18:05.700 --> 18:12.700
So, I select the hex file, I go here and I'm going to flash.

18:12.700 --> 18:23.700
In the meanwhile, I can show you some other outputs like, for example, CAN A status and CAN C status.

18:23.700 --> 18:26.700
In my application, there are only two CANs.

18:26.700 --> 18:32.700
Using these LEDs is possible to understand the status of the CAN.

18:32.700 --> 18:36.700
If the LED is green, for example, the CAN is working.

18:36.700 --> 18:39.700
Otherwise, if it's red, the CAN is not working.

18:39.700 --> 18:48.700
There are other information that we can see using this main layout.

18:48.700 --> 18:57.700
Like, for example, the main current, some temperatures, some pressures and so on.

18:57.700 --> 19:03.700
Now, the flash is finished.

19:03.700 --> 19:14.700
I can, for example, go on key on and then perform the crank and go in engine running.

19:14.700 --> 19:19.700
In this case, I can see the RPMs varying in time.

19:19.700 --> 19:25.700
The velocity in this case is zero because we are not accelerating.

19:25.700 --> 19:28.700
As you can see, here we have the accelerator pedal.

19:28.700 --> 19:35.700
And in this case, the pedal is to zero percentage of pressure.

19:35.700 --> 19:39.700
I can move, for example, go on first gear.

19:39.700 --> 19:51.700
You can see here and then accelerate.

19:51.700 --> 19:55.700
You will see the velocity changing.

19:55.700 --> 19:58.700
You can change gear.

19:58.700 --> 20:03.700
And the velocity is now changing.

20:03.700 --> 20:10.700
The RPMs are changing and everything is working.

20:10.700 --> 20:20.700
Now, another question can be, what if we want to see, to analyze a variable that is not present, for example, in this layout.

20:20.700 --> 20:23.700
We can create a new layout here.

20:23.700 --> 20:26.700
We can insert a new layout.

20:26.700 --> 20:29.700
We will have a blank page.

20:29.700 --> 20:43.700
Then, moving inside, in this case, let me restore the view.

20:43.700 --> 20:46.700
This is another important tip.

20:46.700 --> 20:52.700
It's possible to have something missing on the panels.

20:52.700 --> 20:57.700
You can, every time, reset view set.

20:57.700 --> 21:08.700
And, as you can see, it appeared, here, a new window that before was not present.

21:08.700 --> 21:13.700
So, for example, let's suppose that by mistake I closed this window.

21:13.700 --> 21:15.700
This window is not present anymore.

21:15.700 --> 21:20.700
I can reset here and the window will appear again.

21:23.700 --> 21:31.700
Now, just for example, let's go and search for another variable.

21:31.700 --> 21:36.700
Like, for example, here, an engine variable.

21:36.700 --> 21:39.700
I can take a variable.

21:39.700 --> 21:48.700
I can drag and drop it into the layer.

21:48.700 --> 21:55.700
Control Desk gives you the possibility to choose how you want to visualize the variable.

21:55.700 --> 21:59.700
For example, time plotter, display, and so on.

21:59.700 --> 22:01.700
